\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around six findings (F1--F6) that establish a causal chain from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics and performance collapse. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$), defined as the ratio of a configuration's imagined value to evaluation return, normalized by the baseline's ratio at the same training step.

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 1: Main results summary
%% ============================================================

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Summary of all experimental configurations and outcomes. \textbf{dt\_emb}: temporal embedding mode (trained/untrained/none). \textbf{Sub}: temporal subsampling of training data. \textbf{H}: imagination horizon. \textbf{Eval@final}: evaluation return at final checkpoint. \textbf{NVE}: normalized value-to-eval ratio (peak or final). Configurations with trained $\dt$-embedding and $\nve > 1.5\times$ are shaded.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Exp} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{dt\_emb} & \textbf{Sub} & \textbf{H} & \textbf{Eval@final} & \textbf{NVE} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
001 & Baseline ($\dt{=}1$) & --- & OFF & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\times$ & Healthy \\
006 & Fixed $\dt{=}2$ & --- & OFF & 15 & 132.9 @65K & --- & Collapsed \\
007 & Fixed $\dt{=}4$ & --- & OFF & 15 & 152.5 @65K & --- & Collapsed \\
005 & Learned gate & trained & ON & 15 & 98.8 @145K & --- & Collapsed \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{gray!15}
008d & Factorial (s0) & trained & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\times$ & Full collapse \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
009b & Factorial (s2) & trained & ON & 8 & 267.9 @105K & 0.38$\times$ & Self-correction \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
009e & Factorial (s1) & trained & ON & 8 & oscillating & 1.48$\times$ & Oscillatory \\
008f & No dt\_emb (s0) & none & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.30$\times$ & Healthy \\
009c & No dt\_emb (s2) & none & ON & 8 & --- & --- & Healthy \\
008a & No subsample & untrained & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.59$\times$ & Healthy \\
008c & No subsample & untrained & OFF & 8 & 240.9 @65K & 0.58$\times$ & Slow learner \\
\midrule
008e\_v3 s1 & Mitigation & trained & ON & 15 & 869.9 @105K & 0.80$\times$ & Healthy \\
\rowcolor{gray!15}
008e\_v3 s2 & Mitigation & trained & ON & 15 & 137.0 @105K & 3.78$\times$ & Collapsed (non-CIVO) \\
009a & Baseline (s2) & --- & OFF & 15 & 836.0 @105K & --- & Healthy \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, regardless of mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline (exp\_001) reaches 915.7 at 105K steps and peaks at 970.4 at 505K. In contrast, fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) peaks at 485.3 at 35K before collapsing to 132.9 by 65K---a $-86\%$ decline from peak. Fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at only 176.9 at 25K and never exceeds 152.5 thereafter. The learned-gate configuration (exp\_005) peaks at 541.2 at 55K but collapses to 98.8 by 145K. This uniform degradation motivates the factorial analysis that follows.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves for all configurations. The $\dt{=}1$ baseline (black) reaches 915.7 at 105K; all variable-rate configurations degrade, with fixed $\dt{=}4$ and the learned gate collapsing most severely.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the factor that reliably triggers pathological value overestimation. The key causal control compares exp\_008d (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) against exp\_008f (identical configuration but \emph{no} trained $\dt$-embedding). As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d} (trained): $\nve = 1.78\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $4.12\times$ at 55K $\rightarrow$ collapse (eval 167$\rightarrow$82.8).
\item \textbf{008f} (no dt\_emb): $\nve = 1.02\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $0.30\times$ at 105K $\rightarrow$ no collapse (eval 503.0 at 105K).
\end{itemize}

Using the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold as a diagnostic marker, trained configurations exceed this threshold in 2/2 seeds tested, while untrained configurations remain below it in 2/2 seeds, yielding 4/4 separating power across the factorial grid (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial_grid}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$) trajectories. The dashed line marks the $1.5\times$ threshold. All trained-$\dt$-embedding configurations (red) exceed this threshold; all untrained/no-embedding configurations (blue) remain below it (4/4 separation).}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Three post-CIVO outcomes.}
Running the same 008d configuration across three seeds reveals that trained $\dt$-embedding triggers conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO)---analogous to the delusional bias of \citet{lu2018nondelusional} but arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning, rather than policy-class constraints---yet the downstream consequence is stochastic:

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 3: Three post-CIVO outcomes
%% ============================================================

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Three seeds of the 008d configuration (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) exhibit three distinct post-CIVO outcomes.}
\label{tab:post_civo}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccl}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Peak NVE} & \textbf{Final NVE} & \textbf{Eval@final} & \textbf{Outcome} \\
\midrule
s0 (008d) & 4.12$\times$ & 4.12$\times$ & 82.8 & Full collapse \\
s2 (009b) & 2.05$\times$ & 0.38$\times$ & 267.9 & Self-correction \\
s1 (009e) & 4.12$\times$ & 1.48$\times$ & oscillating & Oscillatory \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent\textbf{Seed 0 (008d):} Full collapse (Pathway A). Value explosion ($+130\%$), evaluation declines from 167 to 82.8, entropy decreases---the classic CIVO feedback loop.

\noindent\textbf{Seed 2 (009b):} Self-correction. The $\nve$ transiently reaches $2.05\times$ at 25K but the value function self-corrects, falling to $0.38\times$ by 105K, and evaluation recovers to 267.9.

\noindent\textbf{Seed 1 (009e):} Oscillatory degradation. The actor loss flips to a positive sign ($+0.011$ at 85K), directly indicating that the value function is misleading the actor. Value standard deviation grows exponentially (5.7$\rightarrow$17.4, approximately doubling per 10K steps), while evaluation oscillates: 82$\rightarrow$205$\rightarrow$113$\rightarrow$174$\rightarrow$121$\rightarrow$185$\rightarrow$103.

These three outcomes demonstrate that while CIVO is deterministic (all three seeds exhibit $\nve > 1.5\times$), the downstream damage is seed-dependent: 2/3 seeds sustain permanent impairment (collapse or oscillation), and only 1/3 self-corrects. The actor-loss sign flip provides a more targeted marker of delusional critic damage than $\nve$ alone, while value standard deviation growth captures oscillatory degradation that $\nve$ misses during ``false recovery'' phases.

%% ============================================================
%% F3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents drift
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3: Untrained Temporal Embedding Prevents Drift}
\label{sec:f3}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_factorial_grid.pdf}
\caption{Factorial experiment summary. Each cell shows final evaluation return and $\nve$ status. Trained $\dt$-embedding is the sole factor that separates collapsed (red) from healthy (green) configurations.}
\label{fig:factorial_grid}
\end{figure}

When the $\dt$-embedding is present but \emph{untrained} (fixed random projection), value dynamics remain healthy regardless of other factors. Exp\_008a (untrained, no subsampling, $H{=}15$) reaches 777.7 at 105K with $\nve = 0.59\times$; exp\_008c (untrained, no subsampling, $H{=}8$) reaches 240.9 at 65K with $\nve = 0.58\times$. Both show value self-correction without the pathological overestimation observed in their trained-embedding counterparts.

This result identifies \emph{training} of the temporal embedding---not its mere architectural presence---as the causal factor. The untrained embedding provides the RSSM with a temporal signal that does not co-adapt with the reward head, preventing the systematic reward-prediction bias that drives CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F4: dt=2 > dt=4 monotonic degradation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F4: Degradation Scales Monotonically with $\dt$}
\label{sec:f4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_dt_amplification.pdf}
\caption{Peak evaluation return as a function of imagination $\dt$. Performance degrades monotonically: $\dt{=}1$ (915.7) $>$ $\dt{=}2$ (485.3, $-47\%$) $>$ $\dt{=}4$ (176.9, $-81\%$).}
\label{fig:dt_amplification}
\end{figure}

The magnitude of degradation scales monotonically with the imagination step size (Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). From the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (915.7 at 105K), fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) achieves a peak of only 485.3 ($-47\%$), and fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at 176.9 ($-81\%$). This monotonic relationship is consistent with compounding model error: larger temporal gaps amplify the RSSM's prediction inaccuracy at each imagination step, and these errors accumulate across the rollout horizon.

%% ============================================================
%% F5: Horizon and subsample secondary
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}
\label{sec:f5}

While horizon length and temporal subsampling affect learning speed, neither is sufficient to trigger collapse in the absence of a trained $\dt$-embedding. Exp\_008a ($H{=}15$, no subsampling, untrained embedding) reaches 777.7 with a healthy $\nve$ of $0.59\times$---a plateau but no collapse. Exp\_008c ($H{=}8$, no subsampling, untrained embedding) is a slower learner at 240.9 but maintains $\nve = 0.58\times$, also without collapse. In contrast, exp\_008d ($H{=}8$, subsampling ON, \emph{trained} embedding) collapses to 82.8 with $\nve = 4.12\times$. The critical difference is the embedding training, not the horizon or data regime.

%% ============================================================
%% F6: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6: Restricting Imagination to $\dt{=}1$ Does Not Prevent Collapse}
\label{sec:f6}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (classic CIVO)---value explosion precedes evaluation decline, entropy decreases. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 seed 2 (non-CIVO)---evaluation crashes while value remains stable, entropy \emph{increases}. Baseline seed 2 (009a, dashed) remains stable at 836.0, ruling out seed fragility.}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

The mitigation experiment exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained $\dt$-embedding for observation conditioning. If CIVO were the sole vulnerability pathway, this restriction should prevent collapse. Results are mixed:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Seed 1} (008e\_v3\_s1): Healthy. Eval 869.9 at 105K ($95\%$ of baseline), $\nve = 0.80\times$.
\item \textbf{Seed 2} (008e\_v3\_s2): Collapsed. Eval drops from 715 to 137 at 105K, $\nve = 3.78\times$.
\end{itemize}

The baseline with seed 2 (exp\_009a) is stable at 836.0 at 105K, ruling out inherent seed fragility as the explanation.

\paragraph{Distinct collapse mechanisms.}
The collapse dynamics of 008e\_v3\_s2 differ qualitatively from the classic CIVO observed in 008d (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}):

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d (CIVO, Pathway A):} value explosion ($+130\%$) $\rightarrow$ evaluation decline $\rightarrow$ entropy decreases.
\item \textbf{008e\_v3\_s2 (non-CIVO, Pathway B):} evaluation crashes ($-81\%$) $\rightarrow$ value remains stable $\rightarrow$ entropy \emph{increases} ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$).
\end{itemize}

In Pathway A, the critic drives collapse through systematic overestimation; in Pathway B, the actor destabilizes independently while the critic remains well-calibrated. Both pathways require the trained $\dt$-embedding, but only Pathway A operates through the $\dt > 1$ imagination channel. This partial falsification of C6 broadens the diagnostic: the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms, not solely through the value overestimation feedback loop.

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 2: RSSM Fidelity
%% ============================================================

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{RSSM prediction fidelity at $\dt{=}1$ vs.\ $\dt{=}2$. The trained $\dt$-embedding (008d) produces a $2.78\times$ reward-prediction gap, providing the systematic bias that drives critic overestimation. Removing the embedding (008f) eliminates this gap but severely degrades absolute reward prediction.}
\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{obs\_MSE}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{reward\_MAE}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7}
\textbf{Config} & $\dt{=}1$ & $\dt{=}2$ & Gap & $\dt{=}1$ & $\dt{=}2$ & Gap \\
\midrule
Baseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\times$ \\
008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & 2.78$\times$ \\
008f (no emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{RSSM fidelity analysis.}
Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} reveals the mechanism underlying CIVO. The trained $\dt$-embedding in 008d produces a $2.78\times$ gap in reward prediction error between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ trajectories. This asymmetric fidelity means the reward head systematically overpredicts rewards at $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates. In contrast, 008f (no embedding) shows a $1.01\times$ gap---the reward head cannot distinguish temporal scales, which eliminates the exploitation channel but at the cost of severely degraded absolute reward prediction ($0.7469$ MAE vs.\ $0.0568$ for 008d at $\dt{=}1$).

\paragraph{Summary.}
Findings F1--F5 establish that trained $\dt$-embedding is necessary and sufficient for CIVO among the factors tested (4/4 separating power), with degradation scaling monotonically with $\dt$ and other factors playing secondary roles. Finding F6 partially falsifies the hypothesis that restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ is a complete mitigation: the trained embedding creates vulnerability through at least two distinct pathways---value-driven (CIVO) and eval-driven policy instability---suggesting that the architectural coupling between temporal conditioning and the reward head is the deeper issue.

