\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the factor that reliably triggers pathological value overestimation. The key causal control compares exp\_008d (trained $\dt$-embedding, subsampling ON, $H{=}8$) against exp\_008f (identical configuration but \emph{no} trained $\dt$-embedding). As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d} (trained): $\nve = 1.78\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $4.12\times$ at 55K $\rightarrow$ collapse (eval 167$\rightarrow$82.8).
\item \textbf{008f} (no dt\_emb): $\nve = 1.02\times$ at 25K $\rightarrow$ $0.30\times$ at 105K $\rightarrow$ no collapse (eval 503.0 at 105K).
\end{itemize}

Using the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold as a diagnostic marker, trained configurations exceed this threshold in 2/2 seeds tested, while untrained configurations remain below it in 2/2 seeds, yielding 4/4 separating power across the factorial grid (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial_grid}).

